From 5cc7cba64b8571bf4e2664d9a9203a3e226205b6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: barracuda156 <barracuda@macos-powerpc.org>
Date: Tue, 1 Sep 2026 07:38:52 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 40/52] ppc32: state the wasm-import-wrapper frame asserts in
 slot units
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

G5 build error #3 of the 12.4 port (v8_base, code-generator-ppc.cc).
New class: static_asserts that hardcode pointer-size-scaled frame
offsets as 64-bit byte values.  AssembleConstructFrame's wasm
import-wrapper prologue documents where its two pushed slots (central
stack SP, secondary stack limit) land relative to fp; the constants
are TYPED_FRAME_PUSHED_VALUE_OFFSET(1..2), which scale with
kSystemPointerSize, so on ppc32 they compute -16/-20 (constant-pool
frame) where upstream asserts -32/-40.  The pushes themselves are
pointer-size-agnostic and all consumers (wrappers.cc) use the named
constants, so only the asserts need fixing: both branches now compare
against -N * kSystemPointerSize (constant-pool: -4/-5 slots, plain:
-3/-4 slots), which reproduces upstream's exact byte values at 8-byte
pointers.

Validation: cross-g++14 probe TU including frame-constants.h under
ppc32 defines compiles the patched assert block clean AND confirms
the concrete values the G5 reported (-16/-20), with
V8_EMBEDDED_CONSTANT_POOL_BOOL verified ON in the probe environment
(the same branch the G5 build takes); the ppc64 side is arithmetic
identity with upstream's hardcodes (-4*8=-32, -5*8=-40, -3*8=-24).
A multiline-aware static_assert sweep (grep -Pzo — the class evades
line-based greps, which is how the earlier assert sweep missed it) of
code-generator/masm/builtins/liftoff/assembler ppc files finds no
other pointer-size-scaled numeric asserts: the remaining literals are
Smi tags, parameter indices, kExponentBias, the deliberate 0031-style
kSystemPointerSize==8/==4 pair, and kOptionalPaddingSize==0 (arm64-
only padding, 0 on ppc32 by upstream's own #else).  ppcheck balanced.
---
 v8/src/compiler/backend/ppc/code-generator-ppc.cc | 12 ++++++++----
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

diff --git a/v8/src/compiler/backend/ppc/code-generator-ppc.cc b/v8/src/compiler/backend/ppc/code-generator-ppc.cc
index b583198..dbaff5c 100644
--- a/v8/src/compiler/backend/ppc/code-generator-ppc.cc
+++ b/v8/src/compiler/backend/ppc/code-generator-ppc.cc
@@ -3329,16 +3329,20 @@ void CodeGenerator::AssembleConstructFrame() {
         // If the wrapper is running on a secondary stack, it will switch to the
         // central stack and fill these slots with the central stack pointer and
         // secondary stack limit. Otherwise the slots remain empty.
+        // The asserts state the offsets in pointer-size slots so they hold
+        // on ppc32 as well (upstream hardcoded the 64-bit byte values).
 #if V8_EMBEDDED_CONSTANT_POOL_BOOL
         static_assert(WasmImportWrapperFrameConstants::kCentralStackSPOffset ==
-                      -32);
+                      -4 * kSystemPointerSize);
         static_assert(
-            WasmImportWrapperFrameConstants::kSecondaryStackLimitOffset == -40);
+            WasmImportWrapperFrameConstants::kSecondaryStackLimitOffset ==
+            -5 * kSystemPointerSize);
 #else
         static_assert(WasmImportWrapperFrameConstants::kCentralStackSPOffset ==
-                      -24);
+                      -3 * kSystemPointerSize);
         static_assert(
-            WasmImportWrapperFrameConstants::kSecondaryStackLimitOffset == -32);
+            WasmImportWrapperFrameConstants::kSecondaryStackLimitOffset ==
+            -4 * kSystemPointerSize);
 #endif
         __ mov(r0, Operand(0));
         __ push(r0);
-- 
